<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
        Wondering how this theming framework is setup?
        This is the framework inheritance hierarchy:

        ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
        │ Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar │
        └──────────────────────────────────────┘
                           ▲
                           │
              ┌────────────┴─────────────┐
              │ Platform.V21.Theme.MyApp │
              └──────────────────────────┘
                           ▲
                           │
                ┌──────────┴───────────┐
                │ Platform.Theme.MyApp │
                └──────────────────────┘
                           ▲
                           │
                 ┌─────────┴────────┐
                 │ Base.Theme.MyApp │
                 └──────────────────┘
                           ▲
                           │
                    ┌──────┴──────┐
                    │ Theme.MyApp │
                    └─────────────┘

        The framework was inspired by these two sources:
          - https://medium.com/monzo-bank/refactoring-android-themes-with-style-restructuring-themes-15230569e50
          - https://chris.banes.dev/developing-themes-with-style-berlin/

        Although most of the theming takes place inside of Jetpack Compose, some broader
        aspects must be defined here. There are five layers to the theme:

          1. Framework theme: Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar. Used as the foundation
             from the Android Material 3 library. Defines the most fundamental aspects of
             the presentation, and defines a very stock, out-of-the-box look without any
             custom branding.
          2. Platform theme: Platform.V21.Theme.MyApp and Platform.Theme.MyApp. V21
             indicates the earliest version of Android that is supported. This layer allows
             us to specify styles which are specific to particular versions of Android. As
             Android matures, we may wish to use a new style or attribute that is available
             in newer versions of Android. For example, if API 26 started to offer a new
             style that we wanted to use for API 26 and higher versions, we would create a
             custom theme file for API 26+ using resource qualifiers. That style would
             inherit from the older version, like so:

             <style name="Platform.V26.Theme.MyApp" parent="Platform.V21.Theme.MyApp" />
             <style name="Platform.Theme.MyApp" parent="Platform.V26.Theme.MyApp" />

             V21 should always be kept up-to-date with the minimum supported API level.
          3. Base theme: Base.Theme.MyApp. The place to override default styles that is
             common across all platforms. That is to say, if there is a style that supported
             all of the way back to API 21 and above, it should go here. Example of what
             would go in here are things like view and text appearances. Colors should not
             go here, since they may change based on light mode, dark mode, or user-defined
             themes.
          4. App theme: Theme.MyApp. The theme which is applied to the relevant Activity
             classes in the manifest. This layer defines many of the colors on top of all
             of the previous layers. This approach makes it easy to add custom themes since
             only this level of the theme much changes. Thus, branding changes or dark-mode
             support becomes trivial to achieve.
    -->

    <!-- region Platform Theme -->

    <style name="Platform.V21.Theme.PageStatus" parent="Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar" />

    <style name="Platform.Theme.PageStatus" parent="Platform.V21.Theme.PageStatus" />

    <!-- endregion -->

    <!-- region Base Theme -->

    <style name="Base.Theme.PageStatus" parent="Platform.Theme.PageStatus" />

    <!-- endregion -->

    <!-- region App Theme -->

    <style name="Theme.PageStatus" parent="Base.Theme.PageStatus" />

    <!-- endregion -->
</resources>
